      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "8269ec58-e68a-513b-ae88-81f9061777ce",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Igor Sikorsky\n- Products: Sikorsky R-4, Pitcairn XO-61, Kellett YO-60, S-6-A\n- Concepts: helicopters, autogyros, hovering\n- Events: 1912 Moscow Aviation Exhibition\n\nFacts:\n- In 1912, Igor Sikorsky's S-6-A received the highest award at the Moscow Aviation Exhibition.\n- The Sikorsky R-4 was a two-seat helicopter designed by Igor Sikorsky with a single three-bladed main rotor and a radial engine.\n- The Pitcairn XO-61 was one of the last autogyros produced and competed with the Kellett YO-60 and the Sikorsky R-4.\n- The Pitcairn XO-61 was hindered by cooling problems with its rear-mounted engine and by the rise of helicopters, which could hover.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "cd4c0a9c-8276-5bea-aeff-75e691e56ce8",
        "text": "Page: Sikorsky R-4. Sentence: The Sikorsky R-4 was a two-seat helicopter designed by Igor Sikorsky with a single, three-bladed main rotor and powered by a radial engine.\nPage: Igor Sikorsky. Sentence: His S-6-A received the highest award at the 1912 Moscow Aviation Exhibition.\nPage: Pitcairn XO-61. Sentence: One of the last autogyros produced, in competition with the Kellett YO-60 and the Sikorsky R-4, it fell victim to cooling problems with its rear-mounted engine and the coming of the helicopter with its ability to hover.",
